0,Nilo-Saharan languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nilo-Saharan_languages,"The Nilo-Saharan languages are a proposed linguistic family consisting of around 210 Native African languages spoken by somewhere around 70 million speakers, mainly in the upper parts of the Chari and Nile rivers, including historic Nubia, north of where the two tributaries of the Nile meet.",Olu’bo,Nilo-Saharan
1,"Olu’bo Language (LUL) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status... | Ethnologue Free",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/lul/,Olu’bo is a stable indigenous language of South Sudan. It belongs to the Nilo-Saharan language family.,Olu’bo,Nilo-Saharan
2,Olu’bo - lul (Nilo-Saharan) - PeopleGroups.org,https://peoplegroups.org/language/lul/,"Olu’bo (lul) is part of the Nilo-Saharan family and Nilo-Saharan, Satellite-Core, Satellites, Central Sudanic, East, Moru-Madi, Southern class.",Olu’bo,Nilo-Saharan
3,Nilo-Saharian languages - Sorosoro Sorosoro,https://www.sorosoro.org/en/nilo-saharian-languages/,"“Nilo-Saharan and its limits”. In Jean-Marie Hombert and Gerard Philippson (eds.), The Genetic Classification of African Languages Revisited.",Olu’bo,Nilo-Saharan
4,AFRICA | 101 Last Tribes - Olubo people,https://www.101lasttribes.com/tribes/olubo.html,"They speak Olu'bo, a Nilo-Saharan language. The population of this group may exceed 10,000. Most persons in this minority are not Muslims, although some are.",Olu’bo,Nilo-Saharan
5,Oluʼbo language — Wikipedia Republished // WIKI 2,https://wiki2.org/en/Oluʼbo_language,Language family. Nilo-Saharan? Central Sudanic.Olu’bo. Opuo. Shilluk.,Olu’bo,Nilo-Saharan
6,(PDF) Nilo-Saharan Languages,https://www.researchgate.net/publication/304040925_Nilo-Saharan_Languages,"Nilo-Saharan is essentially Greenberg's earlier Macro-Sudanic (or Chari-Nile) family, together with languages and language groups formerly assigned to historically isolated units.",Olu’bo,Nilo-Saharan
